/*
*	Sonic 3 & Knuckles Co-op Mod
*	coop_specialstage.lemon  —  Blue Spheres Special Stage (Etapa 9)
*
*	ARCHITECTURE SUMMARY
*	  The Blue Spheres stage is a monolithic "pull" simulation:
*	    - All world state (grid position, direction, speed) lives in shared
*	      global RAM: bluespheres.position.x/y (0xffffe422/24),
*	      bluespheres.direction (0xffffe426), bluespheres.movement_speed
*	      (0xffffe428), etc.
*	    - The SpecialStage() while-loop (fn0081c6) drives everything per frame.
*	    - P2/Tails is a *visual follower* object; its fn00927a update reads the
*	      same world state as P1 and draws Tails a few steps behind in 3D.
*	    - Sphere-at-position checks (fn009402) read P1's grid coordinates.
*	      There is no independent P2 sphere grid in the original engine.
*
*	CO-OP CHANGES (this file)
*	  1. fn00937c override: P2 uses pad2 directly and immediately, without
*	     the delay-buffer / "follow P1" fallback of the original.
*	     Effect: P2 can steer Tails' running direction independently and
*	     choose when to jump/side-step blue spheres that P1 may have missed.
*
*	  2. fn009212 override: reset per-BS-visit elimination flags so a new
*	     Special Stage visit starts with both players active.
*
*	LIMITATIONS (deferred to future work)
*	  - No split viewport: both players share the same 3D camera (P1's).
*	    To get two independent viewports each player would need their own
*	    copy of the entire bluespheres.* state block, and SpecialStage()
*	    would need to run the simulation twice per frame — a ~400-line
*	    rewrite of the monolithic loop.
*	  - Sphere collision for P2 uses P1's grid coordinates (fn009402 only
*	    reads bluespheres.position.x/y). True independent sphere collection
*	    requires the state-swap architecture above.
*	  - Red sphere elimination (fn00972e) is P1-only. When P1 hits a red
*	    sphere the stage exits immediately for both players (original
*	    behaviour preserved). A separate P2 elimination path would only
*	    be useful once P2 has an independent grid position.
*	  - P2 (Tails) renders using SpecialStage.Tails.targetInVRAM which
*	    always shows Tails' sprite regardless of coop.character.p2. The BS
*	    stage only loads P1 character + Tails VRAM patterns on init.
*
*	Published under the GNU GPLv3 open source software license.
*/


// ============================================================
//  BS VISIT INIT OVERRIDE  (fn009212)
//  Called when the P2 Tails object is set up at BS stage start.
//  Reset per-visit elimination flags before base init runs.
// ============================================================

//# address-hook(0x009212)
function void fn009212()
{
	if (coop.isActive())
	{
		coop.bs.p1.eliminated = false
		coop.bs.p2.eliminated = false
	}
	base.fn009212()
}


// ============================================================
//  P2 INPUT OVERRIDE  (fn00937c)
//  Called by fn00927a (Tails update) every frame to obtain the
//  input for P2's movement.
//
//  Original logic:
//    - Maintains a ring buffer of P1's past inputs.
//    - If pad2 has any directional/button input, sets
//      tails.control_counter = 600 and returns pad2 state.
//    - Otherwise replays P1's buffered commands (Tails follows
//      P1's exact path with a small delay).
//
//  Co-op override:
//    - Always return pad2 directly. No buffer, no follow-P1.
//    - P2 immediately controls Tails' direction and jumps.
//    - D0.u16 receives control.pad2; fn00927a uses D0 as the
//      input word for turn/jump decisions.
// ============================================================

//# address-hook(0x00937c)
function void fn00937c()
{
	if (!coop.isActive())
	{
		base.fn00937c()
		return
	}

	// P2 always drives Tails directly — no delay, no P1 path replay
	D0.u16 = control.pad2
}
